WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.750
There's been a lot of buzz

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:04.220
about dubious chemicals in the environment

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.550
that might contribute to
some pretty frightening stuff

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:11.380
like obesity, breast
cancer, thyroid issues.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.837
And they seem to lurk everywhere:

00:00:13.837 --> 00:00:18.837
in pesticides, cosmetics, and
especially plastic packaging.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.720
In light of this,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.200
it's understandable to start panicking.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.440
But before you do,

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.270
let's hear what molecular
biologist Bruce Blumberg

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.950
has to say on the subject.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.170
He's been studying the link
between synthetic chemicals

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.720
and obesity for around 15 years.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.760
So he might be able to
give us a better idea

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.740
of what's really going on

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.163
and what to do about it.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.530
Bad news is, Blumberg confirmed that, yep,

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.830
we're surrounded by these chemicals.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.430
One of the most well known is BPA,

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.000
or Bisphenol A,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.100
which shows up in water
bottles, cans, milk cartons,

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.430
and more.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.940
Bruce: You get them from
thermal paper receipts.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.490
Like I have,

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:07.848
I have these receipts from a recent trip.

00:01:07.852 --> 00:01:10.260
All of these things are
coated with Bisphenol A.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.650
It goes right into your skin.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.410
Narrator: BPA has been
making headlines for years

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.616
about whether or not it's harming us.

00:01:18.616 --> 00:01:21.670
And while FDA-funded
and independent studies

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.080
have conflicting conclusions,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.440
the bigger heart of the issue is this:

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.280
BPA is hard to get away from

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.450
because it's a key building
block in the tough,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.870
clear, flexible plastic
called polycarbonate.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.160
And that proximity to food
is what concerns scientists

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:40.290
like Blumberg.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.530
You don't want to store food in plastics

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.930
because some fraction of those plastics

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.440
will leach into your food.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.050
Narrator: The BPA molecules
that make up plastic

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.180
are bound together by
what's called an ester bond,

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.060
which is extremely sensitive to heat.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.680
So when you heat up your food in plastic,

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.600
that heat breaks some of the bonds,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.240
releasing the chemicals into your food.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.840
A survey by the CDC of 2,517 people

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.450
estimated that over 90% of Americans

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.380
have detectable levels
of BPA in their urine.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.900
And BPA isn't alone.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.250
Phthalates, which make plastic flexible,

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.770
can also leach into food when heated.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:24.220
And reviews of hundreds
of studies have linked BPA

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.830
and phthalates to heart disease, obesity,

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.730
and Type 2 diabetes.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.520
Not to mention a 2015
review linked phthalates

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.460
with impaired neurological
development in children,

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.200
which in 2018 prompted the
American Academy of Pediatrics

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.670
to announce that families should

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.000
avoid plastic food containers entirely.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.320
And other studies on animals,
like mice and monkeys,

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.970
have shown that these
chemicals can lead to problems

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.950
in the lungs, brains,
and reproductive organs.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.300
This is about the time someone like me

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.170
would probably panic.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.580
But there are ways you
can avoid these chemicals.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.740
Blumberg suggests it's best to just stick

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.750
to heating your food in
anything but plastic.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.680
Now, quick aside, BPA-free
plastic containers aren't

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:11.513
the answer

00:03:11.513 --> 00:03:15.150
because research suggests
that BPS and BPF,

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.830
the most common replacements for BPA,

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.510
might have similar effects on your body.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:23.850
Instead, opt for replacements like ceramic

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.697
or glass containers.

00:03:25.697 --> 00:03:27.800
Bruce: You have to do the best you can,

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.120
and it makes sense to me to do the things

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.120
that give you the most
return for the least effort.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.740
Narrator: And here's the best part.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.400
Once you reduce exposure,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.360
those chemicals slowly leave your body.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.420
As Blumberg says, they're
stored in fat cells,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.950
which eventually die and
ultimately leave your system.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.810
Bruce: Don't stress about it, right?

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.400
Do your best

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.173
and make conscious
choices to improve things.

